{"batchcomplete":true,"query":{"pages":[{"pageid":78523860,"ns":0,"title":"Eilenberg–Watts theorem","extract":"In mathematics, specifically homological algebra, the Eilenberg–Watts theorem tells when a functor between the categories of modules is given by an application of a tensor product. Precisely, it says that a functor \n  \n    \n      \n        F\n        :\n        \n          \n            M\n            o\n            d\n          \n          \n            R\n          \n        \n        →\n        \n          \n            M\n            o\n            d\n          \n          \n            S\n          \n        \n      \n    \n    {\\displaystyle F:\\mathbf {Mod} _{R}\\to \\mathbf {Mod} _{S}}\n  \n is additive, is right-exact and preserves coproducts if and only if it is of the form \n  \n    \n      \n        F\n        ≃\n        −\n        \n          ⊗\n          \n            R\n          \n        \n        F\n        (\n        R\n        )\n      \n    \n    {\\displaystyle F\\simeq -\\otimes _{R}F(R)}\n  \n.","description":"Theorem in algebra","descriptionsource":"local"}]}}